# What NOT to upload when deploying from the Vercel CLI.
#
# The CLI does not read .gitignore, so without this file it tries to upload the
# entire working tree — 13 GB here, most of it agent worktrees — and the upload
# aborts with a bare "Upload aborted" that says nothing about size.
#
# Vercel installs dependencies and builds on its own machines, so none of this
# needs to travel. Git-linked deploys ignore this file entirely; it only matters
# for `vercel deploy` from a laptop.

node_modules
.next
out
dist

# Agent worktrees: 9 GB of full repo copies, each with its own node_modules.
.claude/worktrees

.git
*.log
coverage
playwright-report
test-results

# Never upload local environment files. Vercel holds its own copies, and a
# .env.local landing in a build would override them.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example

# Local import staging. LOCAL_IMPORTS_DIR defaults here, so this fills up with
# real manuscripts as authors import them. They belong in Supabase storage, not
# in a build upload.
.uploads

# Python virtualenvs. scripts/autoresearch/.venv alone was 47 MB of installed
# packages; Vercel builds Node, not Python, so none of it is used.
.venv

# Local agent/tooling state, not application code.
.gstack
.swarm
.claude-flow
.ds-sync
.design-sync

# Opus MT weights. `model.bin` is a 283 MB local download that is deliberately
# not in the repo — the code says so in three places, it is untracked in git,
# translation runs through Anthropic and the Railway worker instead, and a file
# this size could not be bundled into a serverless function anyway.
#
# Left in, it was the whole of a 292 MB upload, which is why `vercel deploy`
# kept dying with a bare "fetch failed" partway through on a laptop connection.
# Without it the upload is a few MB. Found 2026-09-02, after three failed
# deploys.
model.bin

.DS_Store
